course: dictionary
lesson: 13668
title: Informing: telling people facts
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To inform someone is to tell them facts or news. «We are writing to inform you that the school trip is on Friday.» The letter gives the parents important news, so inform means to tell someone something they need to know.
explain: Informing is the -ing form of inform, so it shows the action happening right now: «The class captain is informing the teacher about the broken window.» Other forms are inform, informs and informed. Look at the words around it to check you have the right meaning.
explain: Sometimes informing means telling the police or someone in charge about something wrong. «He was informing on the boys who had taken the bikes.» The clue is the bikes being taken: informing on them means telling someone in charge what they did wrong.
fact: To inform means to tell someone facts or news, especially in an official way.
fact: Informing is the -ing form of inform: inform, informs, informed, informing.
fact: Informing on someone means telling the police or someone in charge about something wrong that they did.
fact: Telling a trusted adult about danger or bullying is not telling tales; it keeps people safe.
fact: Phrases to know: «informing you that», «keep someone informed».
exercise: «We are writing to inform you that the trip is cancelled.» What does inform mean here? | To hide something from someone | To sing a happy song | *To tell someone facts or news | To run away quickly
exercise: «Sam was informing the teacher that the lunch hall had flooded.» What is Sam doing? | *Telling the teacher the facts | Cooking the lunch | Playing football at break | Sleeping at his desk
exercise: Which phrase means keeping someone up to date with news? | Keep someone in the dark | Keep someone waiting in the rain | Keep someone busy | *Keep someone informed
test: «He was informing on the boys who had taken the bikes.» What does informing on mean here? | *Telling the police or someone in charge about the wrong things they did | Riding the bikes home | Waving to the boys as they passed | Buying new bikes for the boys
test: Maya saw a boy being bullied at lunch. What is the right thing to do? | Say nothing, because telling is wrong | *Inform a trusted adult, such as a teacher | Laugh along so the bully stays happy | Hide the bullying and never mention it
exam: Which word is the -ing form of inform? | informed | informs | information | *informing
